{
	"id": "g5.maths.multiply_whole",
	"lesson": [
		{
			"text": "Quick memory: last unit, decimals to thousandths. What is the 8 worth in 2.358? 8 thousandths."
		},
		{
			"text": "To multiply 342 × 25, split both numbers into their places. The area model shows every part: 300, 40 and 2, each times 20 and times 5.",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "area_model",
				"cols": [
					"300",
					"40",
					"2"
				],
				"rows": [
					"20",
					"5"
				],
				"cells": [
					[
						"6,000",
						"800",
						"40"
					],
					[
						"1,500",
						"200",
						"10"
					]
				]
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "Add all the parts: 6,000 + 800 + 40 + 1,500 + 200 + 10 = 8,550. So 342 × 25 = 8,550."
		},
		{
			"text": "The standard way writes the same parts in columns: first 342 × 5 = 1710, then 342 × 20 = 6840 (the 0 holds the ones place), then add them: 8550.",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "column",
				"rows": [
					"342",
					"× 25",
					"1710",
					"6840",
					"8550"
				],
				"lines": [
					1,
					3
				]
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "Estimate first, to check: 342 × 25 is about 300 × 25 = 7,500. 8,550 is close, so it makes sense. An answer like 855 or 85,500 would not."
		},
		{
			"text": "Try it in the world: build 25 rows of 342 blocks? That is 8,550 blocks! Estimate first: about 300 × 25, which is 7,500. Next: dividing by two-digit numbers."
		}
	],
